<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<ContentView xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="CardiTrack.Mobile.Controls.QuickActionRow">

    <!-- The four ways to act on a CardiMember, lifted out of DashboardPage so the alert detail
         screen offers the same four rather than a second, differently-worded set of its own.

         Quick actions (issue #162): Call and Message reach the CardiMember's own phone directly.
         SOS is visually distinct (tinted, not a plain white tile) precisely because it dials
         someone else, not the member this row is about — its label dropped the member's name
         (issue: names vary in length once more than one CardiMember can appear). Each tile dims
         independently when its own number is missing (issue #67's dim-not-hide pattern) — they
         stay tappable so the tap can explain why, the only affordance a dimmed tile has on touch.
         SOS leads the row: it is the one action with a deadline on it, and the first tile is where
         a thumb lands and where the eye starts. The others are reachable in the seconds that
         emergency is not.

         Inset from this view's own bounds by the shadow's reach. Android clips a ContentView's
         children to its bounds and the grid fills it exactly, so whatever the tiles cast outside
         the grid is sliced off and reads as a rendering fault rather than as depth. The first fix
         gave the row a horizontal gutter only, which left the same hard cut along the top and
         bottom of all four tiles. The gutter is now the shadow's own geometry (QuickActionTile:
         radius 10, offset 0,3) — 10 on three sides, 13 below to carry the offset — and the call
         sites pull the whole gutter back out with a negative margin on all four sides, so the
         tiles line up with the text above them and only the shadow lives in the inset. Cancelling
         the vertical gutter alone left the row 10 narrower than everything around it on both
         edges, which read as a row that had been centred rather than one that spanned. -->

    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,*,*,*" ColumnSpacing="10" Margin="10,10,10,13">
        <Border x:Name="EmergencyCallAction"
                Grid.Column="0"
                Style="{StaticResource QuickActionTile}"
                BackgroundColor="{StaticResource QuickActionTintRed}"
                SemanticProperties.Description="SOS: call emergency contact"
                SemanticProperties.Hint="Dials the emergency contact number, not the CardiMember directly">
            <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="OnEmergencyCallTapped"/>
            </Border.GestureRecognizers>
            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="10">
                <!-- The same handset as Call, in the destructive red rather than the
                     brand blue: this tile dials the emergency contact, and it was the
                     only thing on it still saying "ordinary call" while the tint and
                     the label both said otherwise. DangerRed clears 3:1 on the red
                     tint (3.97) where the label's StatusRed does not (2.96). -->
                <Image Source="icon_action_sos.svg"
                       WidthRequest="22" HeightRequest="22"
                       HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                <Label Text="SOS"
                       Style="{StaticResource QuickActionLabel}"
                       TextColor="{StaticResource StatusRed}"
                       LineBreakMode="TailTruncation"/>
            </VerticalStackLayout>
        </Border>
        <Border x:Name="CallAction"
                Grid.Column="1"
                Style="{StaticResource QuickActionTile}"
                BackgroundColor="{StaticResource QuickActionTint}">
            <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="OnCallTapped"/>
            </Border.GestureRecognizers>
            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="10">
                <Image Source="icon_action_call.svg"
                       WidthRequest="22" HeightRequest="22"
                       HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                <Label Text="Call"
                       Style="{StaticResource QuickActionLabel}"
                       LineBreakMode="TailTruncation"/>
            </VerticalStackLayout>
        </Border>
        <Border x:Name="MessageAction"
                Grid.Column="2"
                Style="{StaticResource QuickActionTile}"
                BackgroundColor="{StaticResource QuickActionTint}">
            <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="OnMessageTapped"/>
            </Border.GestureRecognizers>
            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="10">
                <Image Source="icon_action_sms.svg"
                       WidthRequest="22" HeightRequest="22"
                       HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                <Label Text="Message" Style="{StaticResource QuickActionLabel}"/>
            </VerticalStackLayout>
        </Border>
        <Border Grid.Column="3"
                Style="{StaticResource QuickActionTile}"
                BackgroundColor="{StaticResource QuickActionTint}">
            <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="OnViewDetailsTapped"/>
            </Border.GestureRecognizers>
            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="10">
                <!-- A member card, not the bar chart: that glyph belongs to Key Metrics, which
                     sits four rows below this tile on the same card, and two controls wearing
                     the same icon on one screen is two controls that look like one. Details
                     opens the person; Key Metrics opens their numbers. -->
                <Image Source="icon_action_profile.svg"
                       WidthRequest="22" HeightRequest="22"
                       HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                <Label Text="Details" Style="{StaticResource QuickActionLabel}"/>
            </VerticalStackLayout>
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</ContentView>
